## A fact that makes you re-examine Tencent

In the past 10 years, Tencent’s stock price has followed a curve that makes people question life:

- At the beginning of 2018, it reached **475 Hong Kong dollars** and was ridiculed as "too high";
- At the end of 2018, it fell to **252 Hong Kong dollars** and was ridiculed as "the game is over";
- It soared to **775 Hong Kong dollars** at the beginning of 2021 and was called "China FAANG";
- It fell to **199 Hong Kong dollars** in October 2022, and was called "the end of the Internet era";
- Returning to more than **400 Hong Kong dollars** in 2024, I was lamented that "I would have bought the bottom if I had known";
- In April 2026, it fell to **467 Hong Kong dollars**, and was criticized as "AI lags behind bytes".

**The same company has been recharacterized by the market at least 5 times in 10 years. ** And every characterization is almost always wrong in hindsight.

Tencent is not an under-researched company - it is one of the most thoroughly researched Chinese companies in the world, with dozens of sell-side research reports covering it every quarter. But more research ≠ understanding. This company has been seriously misunderstood precisely because it is too complex - a 5,000-word sell-side research report cannot fit in it, and it only leaves a binary conclusion of "long/short".

So what this series wants to do is to **dismantle Tencent layer by layer** so that you can see its true structure.

