## Three common misconceptions about Tencent

Before we start to dismantle, let’s dispel three of the most widespread misconceptions.

### Illusion 1: Tencent is a "social company"

This is the most intuitive understanding and also the most inaccurate.

Opening the 2025 financial report, the distribution of Tencent’s revenue of 751.8 billion yuan is as follows:

| Sector | Revenue (100 million yuan) | Proportion |
|------|-------------|------|
| Value-added services (including games + social subscriptions) | 3,693 | 49% |
| Fintech and corporate services | 2,294 | 31% |
| Online Advertising | 1,450 | 19% |
| Others | 81 | 1% |

In the revenue structure, pure "social" (music/video membership) only contributed 17%. **Tencent's real business is to use the social relationship chain as its foundation and build four buildings on top of it: games, advertising, payment, and cloud services. **

But it is the building, not the foundation, that really makes the big money.

### Illusion 2: Tencent is a "game company"

Games will contribute 241.6 billion yuan in revenue in 2025, with a gross profit margin of 60-70%. It is indeed Tencent’s largest profit engine. But if you regard Tencent as a "game company", you will seriously underestimate another company hidden in Tencent's accounts-a 1.04 trillion yuan external investment portfolio**.

Let me make one thing clear first to avoid misunderstandings: the equity assets held by Tencent are divided into two categories -

- **Consolidated subsidiaries** (such as Riot Games 100%, Miniclip, China Literature) - their revenue and profits have been merged into Tencent's main business statements, and **cannot be added separately** during valuation, otherwise it will be a double calculation.
- **Unconsolidated external investments** (financial investments/associates) - this is the real "extra" asset.

In Tencent's financial report, "financial assets measured at fair value + investments in associates/joint ventures" add up to approximately **1,015.8 billion** (approximately 1.04 trillion, Tencent's 2025 annual report data), and **only counts the second category**. Penetrate to specific companies:

- Pinduoduo: About **13-14%** (continued to be diluted by PDD's additional ESOP issuance, 2024 annual report caliber is 15.6%, recent data from Futu and other channels is about 13.76%), equity is about **20 billion US dollars**
- ByteDance: Tencent is one of the early investors. **The shareholding ratio has not been publicly disclosed**. After being diluted by multiple rounds of new financing, industry estimates are in the single digits. The valuation of a single equity ** is much smaller than the US$50 billion circulated in the market**
- Spotify: 9.1%, equity approximately **$9.3 billion**
- Sea Limited: 18.7%, equity approximately **US$8.6 billion**
- Universal Music, WeBank, Epic Games (28%), Kuaishou, Supercell, Meituan, Krafton…

**When you buy one share of Tencent, you simultaneously own the equity segments of the long list of companies above. ** Valuing Tencent as a pure gaming company is equivalent to throwing away this 1 trillion "external investment portfolio" in vain - this is the key mathematical root of the market's long-term discount to Tencent.

(Note: The revenue and profits from League of Legends/Valorant, which is 100% owned by Riot Games, have been reflected in Tencent’s “game” business reports, and are not within the 1 trillion “investment portfolio” caliber above - it is another layer of superimposed value, which will be discussed separately in Parts 03 and 04.)

### Illusion 3: Tencent "lost" in the AI era

This is the hottest narrative of 2026. The reason is:

- Yuanbao (Tencent's AI assistant) MAU 57.35 million (calculated by third parties such as QuestMobile, as of early 2026);
- Doubao (Byte’s AI assistant) MAU 345 million (same caliber in the same period);
- **The magnitude difference is about 6 times**.

The data is correct, but the conclusions are questionable.

If you think the AI era is equal to the "AI chat assistant MAU battle", then Tencent is indeed lagging behind. But if you think that the AI era is equal to "Who can turn AI into a multiplier for existing business?", the conclusion is exactly the opposite:

- Tencent's advertising business will grow **+19%** in 2025 (financial report data), among which the AIGC tool "Miaosi" penetrates a higher proportion of customers (third-party research estimates about 65%);
- The GMV of WeChat stores in 2024 will be **+192%** year-on-year (disclosed in WeChat public courses). In 2025, Tencent will shift to structural indicator disclosure (the GMV growth rate of brand sales is 4.3 times that of the platform as a whole, and monthly mobile sales merchants +1.7 times - see article 02 for details) - AI customer service, AI product selection, and AI translation are almost standard;
- The average daily search volume on WeChat Souyisou increased from 333 million to **1 billion+** (The Paper);
- The monetization rate of video account advertising is significantly lower than that of Douyin - a third-party estimate is about 1.8% for Tencent and about 6-7% for Douyin (note: the monetization rate is an unofficial calculation, and the calculations vary greatly from different studies), but even a conservative estimate means that there is still room for 2-3 times improvement in monetization of video account advertising.

**Tencent is not trying to be "China's ChatGPT", what Tencent is trying to do is "AI in All"** - mixing AI into every dish of WeChat, advertising, games, and payment. Which of the two routes is better still needs time to be tested (see Part 05 for details), but only using ChatGPT’s standards to evaluate Tencent will miss its AI leverage in existing businesses.

