## To understand Tencent, you need to wear several pairs of glasses at the same time

If you had to define Tencent in one sentence, the closest version might be:

> **Tencent = an operating company that uses WeChat’s social relationship chain to lock in 1.4 billion users + an investment fund with a market value of about 1 trillion yuan + a cash flow machine that prints 230 billion yuan of money every year. **

Everyone has written about these three things separately, but few have evaluated them together. This is one of the common explanations for the market's frequent discount on Tencent's valuation - the current PE is about 45% lower than the 10-year median (about 27x), and the valuation model is not good at handling "three-layer overlay" companies.

To understand this company, you need to wear at least these pairs of glasses at the same time:

| Glasses | What to see | Which part of this series to start |
|------|---------|-------------------|
| Business model | How the WeChat ecological flywheel rotates | Chapter 02 · Moat |
| Business | 14 Everlasting Games + Overseas Breakthrough | Chapter 03 · Games |
| Assets | The true composition of a $1 trillion investment portfolio | Chapter 04 · Investment Map |
| Variables of the Times | Is AI an opportunity or a threat | Chapter 05 · AI |
| Finance | Why gross profit margin soared from 42% to 56% in 4 years | Chapter 06 · Finance |
| People | Ma Huateng and Liu Chiping’s Capital Allocation Discipline | Chapter 07 · Management |
| Decision | How much to buy and what signal to sell | Chapter 08 · Decision |

Wearing each pair of glasses separately, you will see the correct but incomplete version of Tencent.

