## A small preview: Is Tencent expensive now?

Leave a hook and let's end with a set of numbers. These numbers will be explained in detail in Chapters 06 and 08. Remember the magnitude first:

| Indicators | 2025 Data |
|------|------------|
| Non-IFRS net profit | **259.6 billion** (+17%) |
| Non-IFRS EPS | ~28.6 RMB / ~32.5 HKD |
| Free cash flow | **182.6 billion yuan** (still +18% after large investment in AI) |
| Shareholder return (buyback 80 billion + dividend 41 billion) | **HK$121 billion** |
| Net cash | **107.1 billion** |
| Gross profit margin | **56%** (increased from 42% in 4 years, a record high) |
| Current stock price (2026-05-04) | ~470 Hong Kong dollars |
| Current PE (Non-IFRS caliber) | **~14.5x** (HKD 470 ÷ Non-IFRS EPS HKD 32.5) |
| Current PE (IFRS/GAAP TTM caliber) | **~17.3x** (Futu channel real-time data) |
| 10-year median PE | ~27x |
| Portfolio fair value | **~1,015.8 billion** |

> **Data source**: Tencent 2025 annual report (released on 2026-03-18), HKEX disclosure.
> AI data (Miaosi penetration is 65%, Souyisou daily searches are 1 billion+, video account monetization rate is 1.8%, AI comics exceed 100 million, etc.) come from third-party research and calculations (CBNData, Guanchao New Consumption, QuestMobile, etc.). The figures from different sources are slightly different and are only for magnitude reference.

Put all this together, and what you see from Tencent is this:

- A company with an extremely deep moat and record gross profit margins
- Current valuation is **45%** below the 10-year median
- 1 trillion in equity assets lying on the books
- Still taking 120 billion in cash to buy back + dividends every year
- But at the same time, market sentiment continues to be bearish as the AI narrative is taken away by bytes

What do these facts mean together? **Different glasses will give different answers. ** This is the question that the next 7 posts will answer.

